WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.120
One protein can pull an axon toward it, and push another axon away.

2
00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:15.220
That protein is netrin, a secreted protein that guides growing axons.

3
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.320
Here is a growth cone, the moving tip of a growing axon.

4
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.840
Netrin is released from a source on the right, and it thins out with distance.

5
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.560
Which way will the growth cone turn?

6
00:00:29.433 --> 00:00:33.553
The growth cone reads netrin with receptors on its surface.

7
00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:36.733
One receptor is called DCC.

8
00:00:37.813 --> 00:00:42.253
A growth cone that carries DCC turns toward the netrin source.

9
00:00:42.873 --> 00:00:43.913
That is attraction.

10
00:00:45.273 --> 00:00:51.233
In the worm, mutants that lack the relative of DCC fail mainly to move toward the cue.

12
00:00:52.317 --> 00:00:56.937
Now add a second receptor, called UNC5.

13
00:00:57.777 --> 00:01:05.097
A growth cone that carries DCC and UNC5 turns away from the same netrin.

14
00:01:05.997 --> 00:01:07.497
The cue did not change.

15
00:01:07.957 --> 00:01:09.577
Only the receptors changed.

16
00:01:10.557 --> 00:01:17.077
In worms, forcing UNC5 into touch neurons sent their axons away from the cue.

17
00:01:17.867 --> 00:01:20.207
Receptors are only one layer.

18
00:01:20.827 --> 00:01:23.287
The growth cone's inner state is another.

19
00:01:24.147 --> 00:01:28.927
To test it, researchers grew single frog spinal growth cones in a dish.

20
00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:34.907
A fine pipette puffed netrin on one side, and the growth cones turned toward it.

21
00:01:35.687 --> 00:01:39.967
This attraction through DCC needs cyclic AMP signalling.

22
00:01:40.587 --> 00:01:43.987
With that signal on, the growth cone turns toward netrin.

23
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.660
Now block the signal.

24
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:54.140
Use a competing cyclic AMP analogue, or an inhibitor of protein kinase A. The same

25
00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:56.920
growth cones turn away from the same pipette.

26
00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:01.240
An antibody to DCC stopped both turns.

27
00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:03.660
So the receptor stayed the same.

28
00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:07.300
The cell's cyclic AMP chose the direction.

29
00:02:08.183 --> 00:02:11.123
To predict a turn, ask two questions.

30
00:02:11.943 --> 00:02:14.063
Which receptors does the growth cone carry?

31
00:02:14.743 --> 00:02:19.543
DCC alone attracts, and UNC5 receptors add repulsion.

32
00:02:20.263 --> 00:02:22.423
And what is its cyclic AMP level?

33
00:02:23.103 --> 00:02:27.663
In frog spinal growth cones, lowering it flips attraction to repulsion.

34
00:02:28.583 --> 00:02:33.083
Treat this as a starting point, because netrin has other receptors and partners too.

35
00:02:33.803 --> 00:02:36.083
Netrin does not decide the turn alone.

36
00:02:36.083 --> 00:02:39.263
The receptors and the inner state decide it.
